            <ArticleTitle>Hyperammonemia as a Marker of Subclinical Seizures After Traumatic Cardiac Arrest.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e33361</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5812/atr.33361</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This report details the presence of hyperammonemia in a patient who sustained cardiac arrest after a traumatic amputation. Serum ammonia levels may rise due to numerous etiologies; however, few reports detail its usefulness in diagnosing subclinical seizures. In this case, we successfully utilized persistently elevated serum ammonia levels as a marker of subclinical seizures in a patient who sustained traumatic cardiac arrest.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
